 of negative 8 should be equal to  0.  but then we start seeing something  interesting because the inverse has to  be has to be a function in its own right  and if we say well okay what should f  inverse of negative 6 be  and you say well g there's two ways to  get to negative 6 for the function you  could go from 2 or negative 2. so the f  inverse if you're trying to do it you  say maybe it's 2 or it's negative 2.  well then f inverse wouldn't be a  function if one input maps to multiple  outputs so this isn't okay so when you  see something like this when you have a  function that where you have two of its  inputs mapping to the same output  you no longer have an invertible  function so this one as it's defined  right over here is not invertible  not  it is not invertible and why isn't it  invertible because you have two  different values mapping to the same f  of x and this or actually you have that  happening multiple times two  and  negative two both map to six  they both map to six  three  and  three and negative three both map to the  same value in fact this keeps happening  over and over and over again  and so you can start to see well maybe  there's a test for whether something is  invertible and just as we have the  vertical line test for a uh to see if  something can be a function we can do a  horizontal line test to see if it is  invertible and this one clearly fails  that because with the horizontal line  you can see that multiple x values  multiple x values can map to the same f  of x value to the same if we say that  you know this is the graph of if you say  this is the graph of y is equal to f of  x they're mapping to the same y value if  they're mapped to the same y value then  when you try to invert it that y value  will map to two different x values and  so that the inverse or this thing that  you're trying to construct as an inverse  would no longer be a function so this  thing isn't invertible but then it gives  us a question can we restrict can we  restrict this function in some way can  we restrict the domain of this function  in some way so that it could be  invertible and one other way to think  about it could we restrict this function  in a way in some way so it passes this  horizontal line test  well one way that we could restrict this  function one way that we could restrict  this function  is if we restricted it only to  the right two quadrants or only to the  left two quadrants so for example if you  restricted this function to  let's say the domain going from zero to  actually you could go to infinity you  could go to infinity if you like  then all of a sudden the only stuff that  you would have in your function the only  part of your function that would be if  you restricted to this domain would be  this side right over here and if you  only have one side of this parabola  of this of this curve then it would pass  the horizontal line test and it would be  invertible and the reason why it passes  it because then you have a one-to-one  mapping for each  each each y value is being mapped to by  only one x value by a unique x value so  you could restrict in that way or you  could restrict in other ways you could  restrict from you could restrict from  negative infinity negative infinity to  zero i wrote that infinity in a weird  way you could restrict from negative  infinity to zero you could restrict from  negative three to negative two if you  restricted from negative three to  negative two the only part of your  function you would see is this part  right over here which would pass your  horizontal line test and your vertical  line test and so it's a legitimate  function and it would be  invertible so hopefully that gives you a  little bit more sense of what  invertibility means or  or how you can test for it and how you  can restrict the domain of a function so  that you can take a non-invertible  function a non-invertible function and  turn it into an invertible one

